  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132269",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.48) \\\\  & =78 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(150)(0.48)=72\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(150)(0.48)(1-0.48)=37.44.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(72,37.44).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=67).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5) & =P(\\frac{66.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{67.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.90\\le Z\\le -0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z<-0.90) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z\\le -0.90) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.74)-Φ(-0.90) \\\\  & =0.2297-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.046\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈0.046.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0468.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
